Adding functionality for developers to enable a UI for visualizing and querying their SQLite storage layer within their Durable Objects via the Actors implementation. Enabling this functionality requires two pieces:

1. Configure your Actor to allow Studio interactions
```typescript
export class MyActor extends Actor<Env> {
    static configuration(request: Request): ActorConfiguration {
        return {
            studio: {
                enabled: true,
                password: 'secret'
            }
        }
    }

    async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
        return new Response(`Hello, World!`);
    }
}
```

2. Configure the `handler` to enable Studio. This requires "registering" the class name and optionally allows you to customize the studio path
```typescript
export default handler(async (request: Request) => {
    return new Response('Hello, World!')
}, { 
    registry: { 
        'MyActor': MyActor 
    },
    studio: {
        enabled: true,
        path: '/my-custom-studio-path' // <- if not included, defaults to "studio" when enabled
    }
});
```

